  "query_preview": "..The upper level low that brought yesterday's weather\nacross most of Arizona is ejecting off to the northeast this\nafternoon. Still a few residue showers and thunderstorms this\nafternoon over the higher terrain and parts of eastern Arizona but\nthis activity will diminish this evening.\n\nWith the low off to the northeast by Saturday, drier air will\nfilter in and along with a fast moving ridge, expect a break from\nprecipitation along with temperatures close to late September\naverages.\n\nBy Sunday a strong West Coast trough will begin to approach the\narea and will interact with tropical moisture streaming north \nfrom Tropical Cyclones Odalys and Polo with the first shower\nchances moving in around midday. The stream of moisture will\nprovide for overnight shower chances Sunday night into Monday\nmorning with more showers and embedded thunderstorms Monday\nafternoon. With some shear from the incoming trough, some of \nthese storms could briefly hit severe levels with strong wind and \nlarge hail. With the deep tropical moisture over the region, WPC \ndoes have the area under a slight risk for flash flooding on\nMonday. This is a potentially slow moving trough and may not fully\nexit the area until Wednesday morning so Tuesday's weather may be\na repeat of what goes on Monday with numerous showers and embedded\nthunderstorms as well as the continued threat of flash flooding. \nAgain this is a dynamic situation that is slowly coming together, \nbut confidence is growing towards a prolonged rain event Sunday \nafternoon through Tuesday night.\n\nFrom Wednesday onward dry air pushes in as the trough exits to the\neast. At this time it looks like the westerlies will take over\nas the dominant force keeping dry air in and the subtropical\nmoisture out. Look for dry sunny days and crisp nights for the\nsecond half of the week.",
